  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Monday)\nIssued at 1233 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThe heat likely peaks tomorrow with widespread triple digit\ntemperatures and continued humidity leading to widespread heat\nindex values ranging from 105 to 115 degrees Tuesday afternoon.\nHave upgraded the Extreme HEat Watch to a warning for Tuesday and\nexpanded the coverage based on latest trends in the data. A strong\nmid level trough moving through the Great Lakes will force a weak\nfrontal boundary southwestward through the region tomorrow\nafternoon and evening. Low level moisture will likely pool along\nthis boundary across portions of northeast Oklahoma, leading to\nthose more extreme heat index values along with light winds making\nit feel rather atrocious at times Tuesday afternoon. Strong upper\nridging overhead will keep any rain chances along the boundary\nbelow mentionable though an isolated shower or two could be\npossible across far northeast Oklahoma. This boundary will usher\nin northerly winds along with slightly cooler and drier conditions\nthrough mid week, taking an edge off the extreme heat, with more\nnormal late July conditions expected. \n\nThe center of the ridge looks like it will begin to retrograde\nwestward through late week and into the weekend. This will allow a\nfew shortwave troughs to drop southeastward in the northwest flow\naloft on the periphery of the forecast area beginning Thursday\ninto the first part of the weekend. Scattered rain chances will\nreturn to at least the northeastern half of the forecast area\nduring this time. Widespread rain chances will remain limited\nhowever. Upper ridging builds back over the Southern Plains by\nlate in the weekend and early next week bringing a return to more\nexcessive heat conditions to close out July.\n\nBowlan",
